@region header
GHSS auction 19-20 September 2026 · Scott 24 · Type V · mint hinged

@region title
# Scott 24 — 1857 1¢ blue Franklin, Type V

Toppan, Carpenter, Casilear & Co. · first U.S. perforated issue (perf. 15½) · Franklin after Caffieri’s bust. Type V exists because the British-style perforator forced the rows closer together: top and bottom frame lines break, side ornaments are cut away. Issued 17 November 1857 from plates 5–10. The workhorse 1¢ of the decade — drop letters, unsealed circulars, newspapers. Lot card: mint hinged, $140. Notes after [The Swedish Tiger](https://www.theswedishtiger.com/24-scotts.html).

@narrative
  - en-US: |
      Scott twenty-four.
      [pause]
      One-cent blue Franklin, Type Five. Toppan, Carpenter — Philadelphia. The first United States stamp that could be torn from the sheet along perforations.
      [pause]
      November, eighteen fifty-seven. The perforator came from Britain as a roulette and was converted. The feed was too narrow, so they squeezed the rows. That squeeze is Type Five: broken frame lines at top and bottom, ornaments cut away at the sides. Look at the detailed view — that incomplete top is the type, not damage.
  - en-US: |
      Franklin after Caffieri’s sculpture. This was the workhorse of the eighteen fifties.
      [pause]
      One cent paid a drop letter — mailed and collected at the same office, before free city delivery. One cent paid an unsealed circular. One cent paid a newspaper. In cities with carriers, a second cent bought the walk to the door.
  - en-US: |
      Mint, hinged, with some thinning on the reverse — as the card says. Catalog one hundred forty dollars.
      [pause]
      Confirm Type Five at the top ornaments. Then bid — this is Scott twenty-four.

