@region header
GHSS auction 19-20 September 2026 · Scott 113 · G grill

@region title
# Scott 113 — 1869 2¢ brown Post Horse and Rider, G grill

National Bank Note pictorial · first U.S. stamps to show a scene, not only a statesman · G grill 9½ × 13 mm. The rider is the Pony Express in mid-leap — critics said the horse’s pose was impossible; the public kept the romance. Two cents paid a drop letter with carrier service and an unsealed circular, and made up higher rates in combination. Scott U.S. Specialized 2023 used $80. Notes after [The Swedish Tiger](https://www.theswedishtiger.com/113-scotts.html).

@narrative
  - en-US: |
      Scott one thirteen.
      [pause]
      Eighteen sixty-nine. Two-cent brown — Post Horse and Rider. The first United States pictorials. National Bank Note put a scene on a postage stamp, and the country argued about it.
      [pause]
      Look at the horse. He is not galloping. He is flying — all four off the ground. Contemporary critics called the pose impossible. Collectors kept the Pony Express anyway. That leap is the story of this stamp.
  - en-US: |
      Two cents was local mail. A drop letter with carrier service. An unsealed circular. And a make-up stamp on heavier or foreign letters.
      [pause]
      The G grill on the reverse is the eighteen sixty-nine grill — nine and a half by thirteen millimetres. Faint G grills are common; this one is there to measure.
  - en-US: |
      A heavy cancel across the rider, as used two-cents often are. Scott United States Specialized, twenty twenty-three: used, eighty dollars.
      [pause]
      Then bid — this is Scott one thirteen.

