@region title
# Scott 211 — 1883 4¢ blue green Jackson

American Bank Note Company · issued 1 October 1883, the day the domestic letter rate fell from 3¢ to 2¢. Four cents paid a double-weight letter — two ounces inland — and made up registered and foreign combinations. Andrew Jackson in the oval, a small color change from the earlier 4¢ green. Lot card: mint hinged, thin, $225. Notes after [The Swedish Tiger](https://www.theswedishtiger.com/211-scotts.html).

@narrative
  - en-US: |
      Scott two eleven.
      [pause]
      Four-cent blue green. Jackson. American Bank Note. First of October, eighteen eighty-three.
      [pause]
      That is the day the domestic letter rate fell from three cents to two. Suddenly four cents was a double letter — two ounces across the country. This stamp is that new arithmetic.
  - en-US: |
      Jackson in the oval, the old warrior recut for a cheaper postage age. Blue green rather than the earlier green — a color the Post Office hoped clerks would not confuse with the two-cent brown.
      [pause]
      Four cents also made up registered mail and foreign combinations when two and five would not add cleanly.
  - en-US: |
      Mint, hinged, with thinning as the card notes. Catalog two hundred twenty-five dollars.
      [pause]
      Then bid — this is Scott two eleven.

