@region title
# Scott 237 — 1893 10¢ Columbian, Columbus presenting natives

American Bank Note · World’s Columbian Exposition, Chicago. The vignette is Columbus before Ferdinand and Isabella with the people of the New World — the court scene that sold the fair. Ten cents paid a double UPU letter (the UPU rate was 5¢) and the usual registered combination: 8¢ registry fee plus 2¢ domestic postage. Lot card: mint hinged, $95. Notes after [The Swedish Tiger](https://www.theswedishtiger.com/237-scotts.html).

@narrative
  - en-US: |
      Scott two thirty-seven.
      [pause]
      Ten-cent Columbian. Columbus presenting natives. Chicago, eighteen ninety-three.
      [pause]
      The World’s Columbian Exposition. Sixteen denominations, from one cent to five dollars — the first United States commemorative set. This ten-cent is the court at Barcelona: Columbus, the sovereigns, and the people he brought back.
  - en-US: |
      Ten cents had real postal work. The Universal Postal Union letter was five cents, so this stamp paid a double foreign letter. Registry was eight cents; add two cents postage and you have ten. That is why tens turn up on registered covers from the fair year.
      [pause]
      Look at the detailed view — the audience chamber, not a landing beach. Same Columbus story as Scott one nineteen, recast for a world’s fair.
  - en-US: |
      Mint, hinged. Catalog ninety-five dollars.
      [pause]
      Then bid — this is Scott two thirty-seven.

