@region title
# Scott 289 — 1898 8¢ Trans-Mississippi, Troops guarding train

Bureau of Engraving and Printing · Trans-Mississippi Exposition, Omaha. The vignette is a cavalry escort with a wagon train on the plains — the Army’s job on the overland routes. Eight cents, from 1893, was the registry fee; this stamp paid that fee in one label. Lot card: mint, disturbed gum, $140. Notes after [The Swedish Tiger](https://www.theswedishtiger.com/289-scotts.html).

@narrative
  - en-US: |
      Scott two eighty-nine.
      [pause]
      Eight-cent Trans-Mississippi. Troops guarding train. Omaha, eighteen ninety-eight.
      [pause]
      The Trans-Mississippi Exposition. Nine stamps, the West as the Post Office wanted it seen: farming, mining, the river, the railroad, the Army. This eight-cent is cavalry and a wagon train.
  - en-US: |
      Eight cents had a precise job. From eighteen ninety-three the registry fee was eight cents. This stamp paid that fee in a single adhesive — no two-plus-six, no leftover. Registered letters from the fair year often wear this design.
      [pause]
      Look at the detailed view. Soldiers, horses, wagons on the plains. That is the overland route under guard.
  - en-US: |
      Mint, gum disturbed as the card says. Catalog one hundred forty dollars.
      [pause]
      Then bid — this is Scott two eighty-nine.

